  "final_text": "All four steps are done, using only the Kibana tools.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has the fields Carrier, Cancelled, Origin/Dest fields, DistanceKilometers, FlightDelay, AvgTicketPrice, and others, plus `timestamp`, the time field.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-84a32e29 Flights' (id `s4-84a32e29-flights-59f33a496539`) was created with two panels:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart of count by `timestamp` (date histogram).\n   - A carrier panel: a bar chart of count by `Carrier` (terms).\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-84a32e29 Cancelled flights' (id `0d853af6-5dca-4a87-9005-bbbbd2504977`) is an `.es-query` rule in `stackAlerts`, running every 1m. It counts documents where `Cancelled` is true over a 5-minute window on `timestamp`, and fires when the count is greater than 10. It was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n\n4. **Panel edit:** The carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\". I confirmed this by reading the dashboard back. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true` (status `ok`).\n\nMy choices: the carrier panel is a bar chart, and the time panel is a line chart. The rule's query filters on `Cancelled: true` with a `size` of 100.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
